<section class="box-emphasis">
<p class="lt-chem-127131">There is some disagreement on the use of &ldquo;amount of substance&rdquo; to describe the measurement for which the mole is the base SI unit; see &ldquo;What&rsquo;s in a Name? Amount of Substance, Chemical Amount, and Stoichiometric Amount,&rdquo; the full reference for which is Giunta, C. J. <em>J. Chem. Educ. </em><strong>2016</strong>, <em>93</em>, 583&ndash;586.</p>
</section>

<p class="lt-chem-127131">We define other measurements using these fundamental SI units. For example, we measure the quantity of heat produced during a chemical reaction in joules, (J), where 1 J is equivalent to 1 m kg/s . Table 2.1.2
 provides a list of some important derived SI units, as well as a few common non-SI units.</p>

