<p class="lt-chem-127131">Chemists frequently work with measurements that are very large or very small. A mole contains 602 213 670 000 000 000 000 000 particles and some analytical techniques can detect as little as 0.000 000 000 000 001 g of a compound. For simplicity, we express these measurements using scientific notation; thus, a mole contains 6.022 136 7 \(\times\) 10<sup>23</sup> particles, and the detected mass is 1 \(\times\) 10<sup>&ndash;15</sup> g. Sometimes we wish to express a measurement without the exponential term, replacing it with a prefix (Table 2.1.3
 ). A mass of \(1 \times 10^{-15}\) g, for example, is the same as 1 fg, or femtogram.</p>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<p class="lt-chem-127131">Writing a lengthy number with spaces instead of commas may strike you as unusual. For a number with more than four digits on either side of the decimal point, however, the recommendation from the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry is to use a thin space instead of a comma.</p>
</section>

