</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Analytical_Chemistry_2.1_(Harvey)/02:_Basic_Tools_of_Analytical_Chemistry/2.01:_Measurements_in_Analytical_Chemistry" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="Uncertainty_in_Measurements"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-127131" id="Uncertainty_in_Measurements-127131">Uncertainty in Measurements</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-127131">A measurement provides information about both its magnitude and its uncertainty. Consider, for example, the three photos in Figure 2.1.1
, taken at intervals of approximately 1 sec after placing a sample on the balance. Assuming the balance is properly calibrated, we are certain that the sample&rsquo;s mass is more than 0.5729 g and less than 0.5731 g. We are uncertain, however, about the sample&rsquo;s mass in the last decimal place since the final two decimal places fluctuate between 29, 30, and 31. The best we can do is to report the sample&rsquo;s mass as 0.5730 g &plusmn; 0.0001 g, indicating both its magnitude and its absolute uncertainty.</p>

